extraction_notes: |
  - WB4 printed 85-87 (cache p086-p088); printed 85 right column and 86 left
    column checked against 200 dpi renders. Printed 90 (cache p091) is a
    full-page illustration with no text. The class sits under the book''s
    Magic and Man section (printed 71), which holds the African Witch,
    Medicine Man, Rain Maker and Priest; no S.D.C. formula is printed, only
    +20 S.D.C. as a bonus: men_of_arms false, occ_group magic, and the +20 is
    bonuses.pools.sdc.
  - xp_table from the African Priest ladder, printed 160, read off a render;
    lower bounds stored. Level 15 prints 329,401-389,500.
  - Ceremonies: the note on printed 87 says chant, drum and dance rituals are
    exclusive to the priest unless stated otherwise. Granted the 14 rites
    with no other owner stated or that name the priest: the Blessing,
    Divining, Exorcism (usually led by a medicine man, a priest may perform
    it at risk of possession), Warning, Water Doubling (priest and rain maker,
    50 P.P.E. for a priest) chants, Drums of Protection, and the eight dances
    including Close a Dimensional Rift (medicine man, rain maker and priest).
    Not granted: Control Ley Line Storms (rain maker and medicine man only),
    Remove Taboo (rain maker only) and the seven Rain Maker''s dances.
  - The book gives the priest the drum messages (printed 86) and no common
    spells: ability 6 says he knows nothing of spell casting. The drum
    messages are prose (Drum Messages section); no spell row exists for them.
  - P.P.E. "P.E. attribute times two, add 1D6 per additional level".
  - Bonuses: +1 vs poison and drugs as toxins_poisons 1 and harmful_drugs 1;
    +2 vs mind control; +3 vs horror factor; +20 S.D.C.
  - Skills: Basic Math -> Mathematics: Basic, Lore: Monsters & Demons ->
    Lore: Demons & Monsters, Preserve Foods -> Preserve Food, Play
    Instrument -> Play Musical Instrument (catalog spellings). No Hand to
    Hand is granted; the book prices Basic 1, Expert 2, Martial Arts or
    Assassin 3 ''other'' skills, no alignment condition printed.
  - Related: five, plus 2 at level 3 and 1 each at 5, 8 and 12. Medical +5
    stored as the category bonus; the +10 on Holistic Medicine is a note.
    Technical''s +20 applies to lore, literacy, language and writing only, so
    it is a note, not a category bonus. Pilot''s horsemanship +10 is a note:
    horsemanship rows are filed under Horsemanship, which is not granted.
  - JUDGEMENT: secondary skills print as ''three secondary skills at levels
    four and ten''. Stored as none at first level and three at each of levels
    4 and 10. The sister classes print ''four at levels one, five and ten''
    (Medicine Man) and ''four at levels two, seven and eleven'' (Rain Maker),
    so the same reading should be applied to all of them.
  - Money: 4D6x1000 in gold, silver and precious stones, plus 1D4x1000
    credits worth of herbs and tonics. Both are valuables or goods, not coin,
    so no starting_money is stored; see Starting Wealth.
  - Equipment not stored (no catalog row, a dice quantity inside a choice,
    or dress): ceremonial headdress and/or mask, tattoos, paint, beaded
    necklaces, bracelets and arm bands, 1D4 talismans or charms (gifts from
    medicine men; which ones is not stated), ceremonial scepter or club, and
    1D6 throwing sticks or a bow and arrows. Everyday clothes -> clothing,
    robe/tunic/cloak -> robe, ceremonial clothes -> ceremonial-robe, several
    sacks -> two small and one large, three weeks of rations -> 3 (the food-rations row is about a week).
    Armor, vehicle and the two Blessed Weapons are prose.
---

## Lore

The African priest educates, protects and helps his people. Most are skilled holistic healers and the keepers of their tribe''s history, myths and legends, which they present orally through song, dance and ritual; they also act as advisors, comforters and judges. Their strengths are knowledge, the trust of the people, and the ritual magic that protects against bad medicine, enemies, nature and the forces of evil. They lead most ceremonies and alone hold the secrets of the ritual dances, drums, chants and songs.

The position is inherited in some societies and taught to the able in others. Many priests shave their heads or wear ceremonial dress to mark their station. About 60% are male; some societies admit women, and a couple admit only women. Priests, medicine men and rain makers often work together. A player character priest is most likely a wanderer who travels a circuit of villages without priests, healing the sick and fighting witches, bad medicine and evil spirits.

Alignment: any, but usually principled, scrupulous or unprincipled. A high M.A. and P.E. help but are not required.

## Drum Messages

Priests pound out coded messages that other priests and royalty can read, audible about two miles (3.2 km) away: a call to arms against other humans, or against evil spirits (demons or a witch); a call for help naming the trouble (invaders, battle, sickness, locusts, robots, evil medicine, evil spirits); a warning of intruders naming them (rival tribe, whites, bots, evil spirits); warnings of evil spirits in the area, of witchcraft, of great or impending danger, of danger to the king or queen, and of danger to a sacred, taboo or magic place; battle in progress and by whom; and rejoicing, with its cause (a birth, a wedding, a new king, a victory, a witch or demon defeated, a notable''s recovery).

## Ceremonies

The priest works magic only through ceremony, drawing the P.P.E. of focused, trusting participants. He coordinates every ceremony; without him the proper rite and its magic are not possible. A medicine man may channel the energy, and some rites belong to the medicine man or rain maker, but it is the priest who knows the chants, songs and dance patterns. Ceremonies are performed only when truly needed - against evil spirits, witches and monsters, unnatural plagues, droughts and magic - and seldom against ordinary men.

## Starting Wealth

The priest starts with 4D6x1000 in gold, silver and precious stones, and another 1D4x1000 credits'' worth of herbs and tonics. He also carries 1D4 talismans or charms given by medicine men, a ceremonial scepter or club, ceremonial headdress and/or mask, and 1D6 throwing sticks or a bow and arrows.

## Armor and Vehicles

Few tribal people wear cumbersome armor. Priests may wear leaf or bark armor from a Millennium Tree. Many priests walk or ride a horse, ass, camel or elephant; more modern priests, especially on the northern and southern plains, may ride a jeep, truck, motorcycle or hover vehicle.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'african-priest');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'african-priest';

